---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="delta-follow-up">
  <p><a href="/#s6">← Back to the main guide</a></p>
  <div class="num">OPTIONAL FOLLOW-UP</div>
  <h1>Review a change in Delta — with help</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Your website already works. This separate exercise is optional: ask the
    person helping you to prepare it with you, rather than treating it as the
    next step in the main guide.
  </p>
  <p>
    The main guide does not create a Git repository, an <code class="i"
      >origin</code
    >
    remote, or a <code class="i">my-site-delta</code> copy. Delta needs different
    setup, which you will check together below. Do not publish the project just to
    make this exercise work.
  </p>
  <h2>Before installing: check compatibility and the risks</h2>
  <p>
    On a Mac, Delta requires Apple Silicon and macOS 13 or later; Intel Macs are
    not supported. Check Apple menu → About This Mac before installing. On
    Windows or Linux, check the requirements on the linked installation page
    with the person helping you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Delta is an agent app from Zed with a different safety model. Isolated
    Workspace gives a thread a separate working copy; Existing Local Checkout
    edits your selected project directly. Check the Work In selection: a new
    thread can inherit your previous choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither choice is a security sandbox. Delta's
    <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/privacy-and-security/agentic-safety"
      >safety docs</a
    >
    say agents can access the device where they run and call tools without asking,
    including destructive actions. Unlike Claude Code, Delta has no agent permission
    system. Asking for a plan is not enforced approval. An outside backup helps recovery,
    but is not protected from an agent with device access.
  </p>
  <p>
    Project contents and history <a
      href="https://delta.dev/docs/privacy-and-security/data-storage"
      >sync to Delta's servers</a
    > when you add a project, including tracked files and untracked files Git does
    not ignore. Agent requests may also send relevant messages and file contents to
    your selected model provider, not automatically every file in the repository.
    Use only material you are comfortable uploading. Backups cannot undo disclosure.
    Send redacted error text to the person helping you rather than sharing a thread.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are comfortable with those risks and your computer is supported,
    follow Delta's official <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/installation"
      >installation instructions</a
    > with the person helping you, then complete the in-app welcome setup. Check <a
      href="https://delta.dev/docs/agents/models-and-providers">model access</a
    > and costs before paying. Do not assume your Claude subscription also provides
    model access in Delta.
  </p>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Create a separate working copy with help'}>
      <p>
        Close Claude Code, Delta, and any other agents first. Find your checked,
        untouched <code class="i">my-site-checked</code> backup in Finder or File
        Explorer. If it is missing or you are unsure it is a good backup, stop and
        ask the person helping you before proceeding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Duplicate that backup and name the new folder <code class="i"
          >my-site-delta</code
        >. Keep the backup outside the new working folder, for example as a
        sibling beside it, not nested inside it. Do not overwrite an existing
        folder with that name. Leave both <code class="i">my-site</code> and
        <code class="i">my-site-checked</code> unchanged, and never open the backup
        as a Delta project.
      </p>
      <p>
        Delta works with Git repositories, folders with saved change history.
        Its <a href="https://delta.dev/docs/concepts/worktrees"
          >workspace docs</a
        > specify a configured <code class="i">origin</code> remote, another location
        for that history. Ask the person helping you to check and prepare this prerequisite
        on <code class="i">my-site-delta</code>, and review what its files and
        history would upload. If it has only local history or no Git setup, stop
        until they have helped. Do not invent a remote, publish files, or ask an
        agent to set this up for you.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Open the working copy'}>
      <p>
        With your helper, check Settings → Thread → Sharing and confirm Default
        Visibility is not set to Your organization. Do not share this exercise's
        thread.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Choose Add Project.</li>
        <li>
          Select the top-level <code class="i">my-site-delta</code> folder, not its
          photos folder.
        </li>
        <li>Review the cloud-sync notice if shown before continuing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Delta's <a
          href="https://delta.dev/docs/getting-started/quick-start#open-your-project"
          >Quick Start</a
        >
        is a reference if the screen differs. This exercise uses the checkout selection
        and request below, not the Quick Start's task.
      </p>
      <p>
        For this exercise, deliberately select Work In → Existing Local
        Checkout. Check the project menu's checkout path points to <code
          class="i">my-site-delta</code
        > before sending anything. This changes that copy directly, not an isolated
        workspace. Open that same folder's <code class="i">index.html</code>
        in your browser. Run only one agent at a time, with no other threads working
        in this checkout. You do not need Git commands for this review.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'One small change'}
    code={'Read CLAUDE.md. Add "Maker and photographer" below my name in index.html; use style.css if needed. Change nothing else. Do not run Git commands, publish or push the project anywhere. Use only Delta\'s normal project synchronization. Show a short plan and wait for my ok before editing. Then list changed files and checks.'}
    copy
  />
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Review the result'}>
      <p>
        Read the plan before replying; remember that waiting is a request, not a
        permission control. Open Changes with the upper-right diff icon to
        compare additions and removals (a "diff"). Select a line and type
        feedback, or send a normal message. Refresh <code class="i"
          >my-site-delta</code
        >'s <code class="i">index.html</code> in your browser; check the words and
        narrow layout.
      </p>
      <p>
        These edits are already applied, not awaiting acceptance. Leave the main
        website and backup alone. For help, send the person helping you redacted
        error text, not a shared thread.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <h2>If something goes wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Stop and close all agents, including Delta and Claude Code. Keep the damaged
    working folder for inspection; do not delete it or restore affected files
    inside it. With the person helping you, make a fresh working copy from the
    untouched <code class="i">my-site-checked</code> backup, using a new name such
    as <code class="i">my-site-delta-retry-1</code> that does not already exist. Keep
    the backup outside this new folder too. If the backup may have changed, stop rather
    than assuming it is safe.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ask the person helping you to recheck the fresh copy and its Git setup
    before adding it to Delta. Confirm the new checkout path and open the new
    copy in your browser before trying again. Do not resume an old thread
    against the damaged folder or open the backup in Delta.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/#s6">← Back to the main guide</a></p>
</div>
